Basic Linux Process Management
 `ps` – Displays currently running processes.
 `ps -f` – Displays processes in full format with details.
 `ps aux` – Displays all running processes with detailed information.
 `top` – Monitors running processes, CPU and memory usage in real time.
 `htop` – Displays processes in an interactive interface.
 `pidof process_name` – Displays the Process ID (PID) of a process.
 `pgrep process_name` – Searches for a process by its name.
 `sleep 100 &` – Runs a process in the background.
 `jobs` – Displays background jobs in the current terminal.
 `fg` – Brings a background process to the foreground.
 `bg` – Resumes a stopped process in the background.
 `kill PID` – Sends a termination signal to a process.
 `kill -9 PID` – Forcefully terminates a process.
 `pkill process_name` – Terminates processes using their names.
 `nice -n 10 command` – Starts a process with a changed priority.
 `renice 5 -p PID` – Changes the priority of an existing process.
 `nohup command &` – Runs a process that can continue after terminal logout.
 `free -m` – Displays memory usage in MB.

- Process: A program that is currently running.
- PID: Unique identification number of a process.
- Foreground: Process running directly in the terminal.
- Background: Process running without blocking the terminal.
